# tests/recursion/k93.rxt -- K93 (docs/dev/known_issues.md): a quantifier
# inside a SUBROUTINE-CALL TARGET runs under EVERY call site's follow, not
# only under its group's lexical one.
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, NOT GENERATED, `k69.rxt`'s precedent: one fix's regression.
# Every expectation below was read off libpcre2 10.46 BEFORE it was written --
# a light probe on the reference box through the committed ctypes binding
# (docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py, `search` from
# offset 0, default options AND PCRE2_NO_AUTO_POSSESS), transcript in
# docs/dev/lanes/k93fix_report.md. pcrec was not asked. No python arm (python's
# `re` has no subroutine call).
#
# WHAT MOVED. src/opt/possessify.c gave a quantifier inside a called group a
# verdict computed from the group's LEXICAL follow, on the premise that the
# lexical position is where "the enclosing follow is the real one". A call
# re-runs the group under the CALL SITE's follow, so the verdict now has to
# hold under the JOIN of every context the body can run in (the lexical one,
# every call site's, transitively through nested calls and recursion; a call
# inside a lookaround contributes every byte). The same walk is the free
# discharge's survey (src/opt/atomic.c), so the discharge moved with it.
#
# The two 10.46 answers agree on every cell here except the `(?R)` block,
# where 10.46's own auto-possessification is not call-aware; that block's
# note says which answer is which and that the choice is unruled.
#
# Every block is CAPTURE-BEARING, so it routes to the VM, where possessify
# runs; the witnesses are NOMATCH (or a longer lazy span) on main 54c42e36.

# THE K93 WITNESS. `a+` is followed lexically by `b` and at the call site by `a`; possessified against `{b}` it eats the `a` the call site must leave (main 54c42e36: NOMATCH on all three matching subjects).
pattern (a+)b(?1)a
features recursion
m "abaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 2
m "abaaa" 0 5
g 1 0 1
n "aba"
n "ab"

# K93's named spelling, over a COUNTED callee.
pattern (?<n>a{1,3})b(?&n)a
features recursion,named-groups
m "abaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aaabaaaa" 0 8
g 1 0 3
m "abaaaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 1
m "aaabaaa" 0 7
g 1 0 3

# K93: the group's lexical occurrence is in an alternation branch the match does not take; only the call runs it.
pattern (?:(a+)b|x)(?1)a
features recursion
m "xaa" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "xaaa" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
n "xa"

# K93 related item 1, the FREE DISCHARGE: `(?>a+)` must not be spliced out on a verdict from the lexical follow `{b}`, because the call re-runs the cut under `{a}` and the cut is what makes these NOMATCH. Right by accident on the default build (possessify re-marks the loop); `-fno-possessify` answered (0,4) on main -- tests/possessify/run_possessify_tests.sh section 8 runs that build.
pattern ((?>a+))b(?1)a
features recursion,atomic-groups
n "abaa"
n "abaaa"
n "aba"

# The python-style call spelling.
pattern (?P<n>a+)b(?P>n)a
features recursion,named-groups
m "abaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 2

# A RELATIVE call.
pattern (a+)b(?-1)a
features recursion
m "abaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 2

# A FORWARD relative call, before the group's definition.
pattern (?+1)a(a+)b
features recursion
m "aaab" 0 4
g 1 2 3
n "aab"
m "aaaab" 0 5
g 1 3 4

# A call BEFORE the definition: the call site's follow `{a}` is walked before the group's own lexical follow `{b}` is.
pattern (?1)a(a+)b
features recursion
m "aaab" 0 4
g 1 2 3
n "aab"
m "aaaab" 0 5
g 1 3 4

# NESTED: group 1 is reached through group 2's body, whose own context comes from `(?2)` -- the follow `{a}` reaches `a+` only transitively, which is what the fixpoint iterates for.
pattern (a+)b((?1))c(?2)a
features recursion
m "abacaa" 0 6
g 1 0 1
g 2 2 3
m "abacaaa" 0 7
g 1 0 1
g 2 2 3
n "abaca"
m "aabaacaaa" 0 9
g 1 0 2
g 2 3 5

# A call inside a REPEAT: after the callee returns the loop can restart or exit, so the call site's context carries the enclosing loop's first set too.
pattern (a+)b(?:x(?1))*a
features recursion
m "abxaa" 0 5
g 1 0 1
m "abxaxaa" 0 7
g 1 0 1
n "abxa"
m "aba" 0 3
g 1 0 1

# A QUANTIFIED call.
pattern (a+)b(?1)+a
features recursion
m "abaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 2
m "abaaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 1

# The call and the definition in SIBLING branches, with disjoint follows.
pattern (?:x(?1)a|(a+)b)
features recursion
m "xaa" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "xaaa" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "xa"

# A class body: lexical follow `-`, call-site follow `5`, inside the class.
pattern (\d+)-(?1)5
features recursion,classes
m "12-345" 0 6
g 1 0 2
n "1-5"
m "1-55" 0 4
g 1 0 1

# THE `may_end` CHANNEL. Lexically the lazy `a+?` is followed by `b` and the match cannot end there, so the preference collapse to maximal was licensed; at the call site the match MAY end, which is the lazy conjunct's case (main: (0,4) where 10.46 answers (0,3)).
pattern (a+?)b(?1)
features recursion
m "abaa" 0 3
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaa" 0 4
g 1 0 2
n "ab"

# A call inside a LOOKAROUND, which the possessify walk never enters: such a site contributes the TOP context (every byte, may end).
pattern (a+)b(?=(?1)a)
features recursion,lookaround
m "abaa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "aba"
m "abaaa" 0 2
g 1 0 1

# A call inside an ATOMIC group: the cut makes the greedy callee's first exit final, so these are NOMATCH on every build -- the control that the context join does not loosen the cut.
pattern (a+)b(?>(?1))a
features recursion,atomic-groups
n "abaa"
n "abab"
n "abaab"

# RECURSION through a numbered group: lexically `a+` is followed by `$` (transparent), at the recursive call by `a`.
pattern ^(b(?1)a|a+)$
features recursion
m "baa" 0 3
g 1 0 3
m "bbaaa" 0 5
g 1 0 5
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 0 3
n "ba"

# K93 related item 2, `(?R)`: group 0's region is the whole pattern, and the recursive site's follow `{a}` reaches the top-level `a+`. THE UNQUALIFIED LINES ARE THE SOUND ANSWER, which is libpcre2 10.46's under PCRE2_NO_AUTO_POSSESS. 10.46's DEFAULT answer differs -- its own auto-possessification is not call-aware for `(?R)` (it is for every numbered/named call above) -- and is recorded as the `under pcre2-auto-possess` lines; WHICH ONE pcrec matches is UNRULED (docs/dev/known_issues.md K93, docs/dev/lanes/k93fix_report.md).
pattern (?:b(?R)a|a+)
features recursion
m "baa" 0 3
under pcre2-auto-possess m "baa" 1 3
m "bbaaa" 0 5
under pcre2-auto-possess m "bbaaa" 2 5
m "baaa" 0 4
under pcre2-auto-possess m "baaa" 1 4
m "aaa" 0 3
m "ba" 1 2

# STILL POSSESSIVE, and that is the point: both follows (`b`, `c`) are disjoint from `a`, so the verdict holds at every site and the fix must keep it (tests/possessify/run_possessify_tests.sh section 8 asserts the stamp).
pattern (a+)b(?1)c
features recursion
m "abac" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "aabaac" 0 6
g 1 0 2
n "abc"
n "abaa"

# A class callee whose lexical follow `c` is disjoint and whose call-site follow `a` is not.
pattern ([ab]+)c(?1)a
features recursion
m "acba" 0 4
g 1 0 1
m "abcaba" 0 6
g 1 0 2
m "acaa" 0 4
g 1 0 1

# A call inside a COUNTED repeat, before the definition.
pattern (?:(?1)a){2}(a+)b
features recursion
m "aaaaab" 0 6
g 1 4 5
n "aaab"

# Two consecutive calls: the first call's follow is the second call's first set (every byte), the second's is `a`.
pattern (a{1,3})b(?1)(?1)a
features recursion
m "abaaa" 0 5
g 1 0 1
m "abaaaa" 0 6
g 1 0 1
m "aabaaaaa" 0 8
g 1 0 2

# THE FIXPOINT'S SECOND ROUND. Every call precedes its definition, so the context walk (right to left along the spine) reaches group 2 before group 1's site inside it has been given group 1's context `{a}`: one context walk leaves group 2 at `{c}` and possessifies `a+`; the second widens it to `{a,c}`.
pattern (?1)a((?2))c(a+)b
features recursion
m "aaacab" 0 6
g 1 2 3
g 2 4 5
m "aaacaab" 0 7
g 1 2 3
g 2 4 6
n "aacab"
m "aaaacaab" 0 8
g 1 3 4
g 2 5 7


# A-F1 (a) ([ART-POSS-ARMS] rev 2, poss_arms.md 2.3a/8.2): arm A's gate at a CALLED group's (or the root's) end is valued by the call sites' follows too, not only the lexical continuation. Here `a+` is followed by the bypass `(?:\b|)` and the call site then needs an `a` back: greedy on `baa` is (0,3), a loop wrongly possessified on the lexical continuation alone gives (1,3). 24 subjects (<=5 chars) on which the possessive spelling disagrees found by exhaustive search; libpcre2 10.46, NO_AUTO_POSSESS and default options agree on every cell.
# pcre2-only
pattern (a+(?:\b|))|b(?1)a
features recursion,assertions
m "baa" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "baaa" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
m "baab" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "baa." 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "a" 0 1
g 1 0 1
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 2
n ""
n "b"

# A-F1 (b) ([ART-POSS-ARMS] rev 2, poss_arms.md 2.3a/8.2): arm A's gate at a CALLED group's (or the root's) end is valued by the call sites' follows too, not only the lexical continuation. Here `a+` is followed by the bypass `(?:\b|)` and the call site then needs an `a` back: greedy on `baa` is (0,3), a loop wrongly possessified on the lexical continuation alone gives (1,3). 24 subjects (<=5 chars) on which the possessive spelling disagrees found by exhaustive search; libpcre2 10.46, NO_AUTO_POSSESS and default options agree on every cell.
# pcre2-only
pattern (?:b(?R)a|a+(?:\b|))
features recursion,assertions
engine vm
m "baa" 0 3
m "baaa" 0 4
m "baab" 0 3
m "baa." 0 3
m "a" 0 1
m "aa" 0 2
n ""
n "b"
